speechtext:
That is a fair
enough criticism. But where the work of a department like that of agriculture is largely educational and is carried on by officials who are not fixed in any particular locality, the expenditure must of necessity be largely for salaries and travelling expenses. The hon. member (Mr. Caldwell) reminds me of the question of certified potatoes. Well, there are thirty or thirty-five competent men set aside for work in this connection in the Maritime provinces, and their expenses are practically all for travelling. I presume the cost of transportation on the mileage' basis would be the same there as anywhere else but the general expenses would depend on the nature of the locality they were in. I must impress upon the committee the fact that a close check is kept by means of vouchers on all these expenses. The men are not given a flat per diem allowance; it is transportation plus whatever it costs them, and that as I say would depend upon the district in which they were travelling. For instance, in some of the cities of British Columbia and % the west there are some exceedingly fine hotels, and if the officials found it necessary to put up at some hotel in Calgary or Vancouver the cost would naturally be higher than it would be in some other parts of the country. I do not suppose they would take either the worst or the best hotels; they would go to middle class places. But even there there would be a vast difference and their expenses would be greater than if they were travelling in districts where the hotels are of a modest character. One man might [Mr. Stevens.}
take a long trip to Calgary or even to Victoria and necessarily his expense account would be high; it would be higher than the account of some other man travelling somewhere else. Take the health of animals branch; there are in connection with that branch 140 veterinary surgeons and the vote is nearly $2,000,000. Now, outside o,f something like $700,000 for indemnification in respect of animals slaughtered for tuberculosis the rest practically all goes in salaries and expenses. You cannot station a man at every point where the services of an official are required; these men must travel around. I can quite understand the feeling of my hon. friend who says that the expense is enormous. It is enormous; but I do not see how it is to be avoided. The only thing that can be done, and that is what we are doing, is to keep a close check on all expense accounts. An accountant who is familiar with accounts of this sort easily recognizes where padding takes place, and occasionally that occurs in all departments. But I do not think that our men are extravagant; I do not believe that they patronize hotels which are beyond their reach and which they would not patronize on their own account.
